  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">As part of partnership under the Bangladesh Economic Engagement Program (BEEP) fund, IFC signed a corporate agreement with Bangladesh&apos;s central bank called &apos;Bangladesh Bank&apos; on 17 April 2024 to support the institution to implement its &apos;Cashless Digital Bangladesh&apos; project. With Australian support, IFC will provide technical assistance to Bangladesh Bank to create an enabling environment for increased digital finance by working in the following areas: 
&#x200d;- cost of using cash study
&#x200d;- review of merchant onboarding requirements
&#x200d;- review of payment pricing offered by service providers (banks, credit card providers, fintech companies)
&#x200d;- regulatory capacity building and market awareness of the benefits of digital transactions.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">DFAT, though Australian High Commission Bangladesh, entered into a partnership with International Finance Corporation (IFC) under investment named Bangladesh Economic Engagement Program (BEEP) in May 2023. The aim of the partnership is to create concrete opportunities for inclusive and sustainable private investment through targeted IFC advisory and sector upstream interventions. In the reporting period, IFC with Australian fund support, signed a deal with Eastern Bank Limited (EBL), conducted a risk management diagnostic for the bank and developed a roadmap to improve its risk governance, credit risk management, stress testing, asset liability management and operational risk management. It is expected that EBL will have an improved risk governance with opportunities for increased access to funds for micro, small and medium sized enterprises, particularly owned by women by completion of the program in 2027.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through BEEP, Australia has supported the World Bank (WB) in advancing banking sector reforms and bank resolution initiatives led by Bangladesh Bank (BB). Since BEEP began, BB has adopted the Bank Resolution Ordinance (BRO) equipping it with resolution powers and tools for managing distressed banks. Complementary technical assistance has been provided to BB for establishing the Bank Resolution and Restructuring Unit (BRRU) and implementing Prompt Corrective Action (PCA). Support has also been provided for supervisory technology aimed at improving BB&#x2019;s supervision and enforcement functions. The Deposit Insurance Department of BB has been strengthened for timely deposit data collection and faster payouts. BEEP has also facilitated technical discussions on possible reforms of State-Owned Banks, considered highly important for long-term financial sector stability. For non-performing loans (NPL) resolution, Australia has supported BB in drafting a circular requiring regulated banks to develop NPL reduction strategies and in preparing a Distressed Asset Management Ordinance.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Bangladesh Economic Empowerment Program (BEEP), Australia has enabled the International Finance Corporation (IFC) to strengthen the risk management practices and capabilities of financial institutions&#x2014;particularly during periods of macroeconomic stress&#x2014;contributing to a more resilient financial sector. As part of the investment, Eastern Bank PLC (EBL) is among the financial institutions working to strengthen risk management practices. EBL conducted a comprehensive risk management diagnostic and developed a roadmap to enhance its internal systems. This initiative is expected to support the expansion of its SME and women-owned SME portfolios&#x2014;contributing to broader goals of financial inclusion in Bangladesh. Additionally, EBL has strengthened its risk governance related to climate and explored ways to enhance its internal climate risk management framework&#x2014;aligning with broader goals of sustainable finance and resilience.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
